// **The rhizome of an emergent aquatic plant**: `reed.ron`'s
// `root_material`. Phase 1 of
// `Reports/aquatic-implementation-plan-2026-09-14.md`.
//
// **Its own material rather than `grassroot` for the same reason
// `grassroot` is its own material rather than `rootwood`:
// `reinforces_powder`, pointed somewhere new.** Sod holding a dry bank
// together is grass's one emergent consequence; a rhizome mat binding the
// *sediment under a pond* is this species', and it is the more interesting
// of the two because that sediment is the thing the whole phase is built on.
// §1.6 of the plan: a pond holds only over an impermeable floor, and the
// four-row sediment layer that lets a root in at all drinks 231,040 units --
// 6.3% of the pond -- and then stops. A mat through those four rows is what
// keeps them there when something walks on the bank.
//
// The flag already exists and already does this for any `Plant` cell. The
// reed is simply the second species whose architecture puts enough of them
// through a loose layer for it to show, and the first to do it underwater.
//
// One band of four tonal steps, matching `grassroot` and `rootwood` in
// length: roots are barely seen, and a species' `bark_bands` index wraps
// modulo the palette length at render time, so a second band buys nothing.
// Paler and pinker than `grassroot`'s tan, because a rhizome in waterlogged
// sediment is a pale, fleshy thing rather than a fibrous brown one -- and
// because the two are the materials most likely to appear in one frame.
(
    name: "reedroot",
    display: "Reed rhizome",
    kind: Plant,
    // Walk-through and climbable while alive, as `grassroot` and `rootwood`
    // are. Root tissue underground is no more a wall here than a tree's is.
    //
    // Read only alongside `Cell::organism_id() != 0`, so a painted cell of
    // this material is as solid as it ever was.
    climbable: true,
    // Ground, like `grassroot` and `rootwood`: a rhizome mat is the top of
    // the sediment, not something standing on it, and the gnome walks over
    // it rather than through it.
    underfoot: true,
    // Just above `reedstem`: a rhizome is the stout storage organ of this
    // plant and is denser than the stem it feeds, but it is still soft
    // waterlogged tissue and nowhere near `rootwood`'s heartwood.
    density: 0.4,
    colors: [
        (168, 144, 126),
        (184, 160, 142),
        (152, 128, 110),
        (198, 176, 158),
    ],
    // **The point of the file**, as it is the point of `grassroot`. Loose
    // powder touching a `Plant` cell carrying this is held, so a mat through
    // submerged sediment is many small holds rather than one big one.
    reinforces_powder: true,
    // Underwater and saturated -- the least flammable plant tissue in the
    // world, and it should be. A reed bed burns above the waterline and
    // regrows from the rhizome, which is what a real one does and is worth
    // falling out of two numbers rather than a special case.
    flammability: 0.02,
    burn_temperature: 500.0,
    burn_duration: 40,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.15,
    breaks_into: "litter",
    // As `grassroot`: not a load path, and opted out of the cantilever rule
    // by omission. A rhizome mat reinforces the sediment around it; it does
    // not hold a structure up.
    max_unsupported_span: 1,
)
